;; FH-REACT-002 — one shallow prop rule, or one explicit adapter, and the three
;; names the bridge keeps for itself.
;;
;; The shallow rule is stated as a TABLE of exact names rather than as prose
;; about "matching", because every plausible bug here is a name transformation
;; somebody thought was helpful: camelising `person-id` to `personId`,
;; namespace-dropping `acme/id` to `id`, or deep-walking a value into Clojure
;; data. `:shallow` therefore carries a namespaced key, a hyphenated key, and a
;; value that MUST arrive as the identical JS object it went in as — the one
;; assertion a `js->clj` would fail.
;;
;; The three owned names are the other half. `frame` is consumed; `children`
;; crosses to the boundary's trailing-children slot, which is what lets React
;; content nest inside an exported view at all; `ref` is refused outright,
;; because Freehand retired the neutral ref tier and a ref resolving silently to
;; nothing would leave a foreign owner holding a handle that never fills.
;;
;; `:reserved-collision` is the rejection the reserved name earns. It can only
;; arise in the ADAPTER arm — the shallow arm consumed the property — and it is
;; the one case where the same name would be read two ways at once.
{:fh/id "FH-REACT-002"
 :fh/law
 "Props cross by ONE shallow own-property pass matching exact names with values untouched — no camelisation, no deep walk — or by the one explicit adapter that returns the props map; and the three names the bridge owns behave as declared: `frame` is consumed, `children` becomes the boundary's trailing children under the view's own declared children policy, `ref` is refused, and a props map carrying the reserved `:frame` is refused naming the view."

 :error :rf.error/ui-tree-malformed

 ;; The live frame the owned-name rows scope through. A frame is only ever
 ;; created by the host application, so every row that touches one names it
 ;; here rather than conjuring one at the bridge.
 :frame-id :to-react/adapted

 ;; The default arm. `:foreign` is the own-enumerable property table a library
 ;; supplies; `:props` is the map the view is mounted with, by exact name.
 :shallow
 {:foreign {"person-id" 7
            "column-id" "amount"
            "acme/id"   "A-1"}
  :props   {:person-id 7
            :column-id "amount"
            :acme/id   "A-1"}
  ;; The names a transformation would have produced. None of them may appear.
  :never   [:personId :id :acmeId :person_id]}

 ;; A value is carried across, never converted. The probe hands in a JS object
 ;; and asserts the view received THAT object — identical, not equal-looking.
 :uncoerced
 {:prop "params"
  :note "a foreign parameter object arrives as itself; a deep walk would answer an equal-looking Clojure map and fail identity"}

 ;; The adapter arm. The adapter sees the RAW foreign object — every property,
 ;; including the ones the bridge reserves and the ones the projection ignores —
 ;; and returns the one props map. The `frame` property is in the table
 ;; deliberately: an adapter does not displace the bridge's own reading of it,
 ;; so the exported subtree is still scoped while the props map is entirely the
 ;; adapter's.
 :adapter
 {:foreign  {"data" {"id" 11} "unmapped" "ignored"}
  :sees     ["data" "unmapped" "frame"]
  :props    {:person-id 11}
  :note     "the projection is named, localised code at the host edge"}

 ;; The three owned names.
 :owned
 {:frame    {:note "consumed by the bridge; never a props-map key"
             :never :frame}
  :children {:note  "React's content slot becomes the boundary's trailing children"
             :title "Totals"
             :text  "nested"}
  :ref      {:note     "refused — Freehand has no ref protocol"
             :recovery :write-a-wrapper-or-use-a-behavior}}

 ;; The view's own declared policy still decides. A view declaring
 ;; `:children-policy :none` refuses React children with the DECLARATION's
 ;; diagnostic, not a bridge-specific one.
 :children-policy
 {:view  :re-frame.freehand.to-react-views/badge
  :error :rf.error/view-children-policy}

 ;; The reserved-name collision, reachable only through the adapter.
 :reserved-collision
 {:props    {:frame :some/frame}
  :recovery :rename-the-prop
  :names    "re-frame.freehand.to-react-views/person-cell"}}
